0:00:00.150,0:00:03.335
Here is a game you can
explain in under a minute.

0:00:03.385,0:00:06.910
Two players, one board, a
handful of small pieces each.

0:00:07.483,0:00:09.385
You take turns placing them.

0:00:09.435,0:00:13.587
Each turn, either start a new snake
that doesn't touch any other pieces,

0:00:13.861,0:00:15.605
or grow one of your snakes.

0:00:15.839,0:00:18.493
Whoever ends up with
the longest snake wins.

0:00:19.783,0:00:22.114
Each player gets just seven real turns.

0:00:22.164,0:00:26.583
The whole game fits on a coffee
table and takes about 5 minutes.

0:00:29.883,0:00:33.154
So here is the question that
has been bugging me for years.

0:00:33.204,0:00:36.230
If both players play perfectly, who wins?

0:00:36.280,0:00:40.368
It feels like a question a computer
should answer in an evening.

0:00:40.418,0:00:46.274
This video is about why it did not,
what we found when we tried anyway,

0:00:46.324,0:00:48.709
and a move the engine
had ranked only 76th.

0:00:48.759,0:00:53.843
One move that seemed weak at first,
but changed the whole picture.

0:01:05.890,0:01:08.094
Hi, I am Adam, a Czech game designer.

0:01:08.144,0:01:11.256
You may know me for my
games Project L or Drillers.

0:01:11.306,0:01:15.168
But today, I would like to tell
you about one of my earlier games,

0:01:15.218,0:01:18.840
much less known, but no
less interesting: Snekagon.

0:01:18.900,0:01:21.676
This is a story about a
game that didn't sell well,

0:01:21.726,0:01:24.872
a story about a question
I could not answer,

0:01:24.922,0:01:27.105
a story about player psychology,

0:01:27.155,0:01:30.770
and a story about the deep beauty
and mystery of board games.

0:01:32.550,0:01:34.283
The idea came out of thin air.

0:01:34.333,0:01:38.609
I was riding home from a long,
demanding game night back in 2019,

0:01:38.659,0:01:41.695
and I wanted something
completely elementary,

0:01:41.745,0:01:44.404
as natural and simple as a game can get.

0:01:44.454,0:01:49.550
Somewhere on that tram ride I
caught it, and I did not let go.

0:01:51.217,0:01:52.731
The first games surprised me.

0:01:52.781,0:01:55.986
The rules are tiny, but
the board fills up fast,

0:01:56.036,0:02:00.097
and every piece you place is a
threat and a wall at the same time.

0:02:05.990,0:02:10.185
After a playtest at CGE in February
2020, where it was played a few times,

0:02:10.235,0:02:14.449
I wrote this in my diary: "This
game keeps fascinating me.

0:02:14.499,0:02:17.644
An area-control abstract
that lasts eight moves?

0:02:17.694,0:02:18.527
That can't work.

0:02:18.577,0:02:19.872
But it does.

0:02:19.922,0:02:23.844
You can never calculate
more than seven moves ahead,

0:02:23.894,0:02:26.804
simply because there will
never be that many moves left.

0:02:26.854,0:02:31.670
It is about planning and intuition,
and every game is different."

0:02:32.617,0:02:36.097
And the natural question
followed: who wins?

0:02:36.147,0:02:37.817
This game is small.

0:02:37.867,0:02:40.119
Ninety-one cells, seven turns each.

0:02:40.169,0:02:43.697
It has no dice, no cards,
no hidden information.

0:02:43.747,0:02:48.697
So somewhere out there is a definite
answer: either the first player wins,

0:02:48.747,0:02:52.143
or the second player wins, or
perfect play ends in a tie.

0:02:52.193,0:02:56.227
It really feels like a game
that should be easy to solve.

0:02:58.650,0:03:00.687
I pitched the game around.

0:03:00.737,0:03:06.094
In December 2019, Gigamic, the publisher
of Quarto and Squadro, turned it down.

0:03:06.144,0:03:09.750
The strategy part seemed
a bit too light for them.

0:03:10.200,0:03:12.083
I tried to ask what they meant by that,

0:03:12.133,0:03:16.360
or which winning strategies they
found, but never heard back.

0:03:17.433,0:03:22.633
In the end I published it with a small
Czech publisher Maty Moves in 2020.

0:03:24.833,0:03:27.496
The reception was
lukewarm, to put it kindly.

0:03:27.546,0:03:31.396
And one comment kept coming
back, in different words:

0:03:31.446,0:03:34.983
the first player has an
advantage you cannot beat.

0:03:35.967,0:03:40.387
First player advantage seems to take the
legs out from underneath this design.

0:03:40.867,0:03:43.033
Going first gave me a
near-unbeatable advantage.

0:03:43.083,0:03:46.269
Maybe if player one doesn't
take the center space...

0:03:46.319,0:03:47.624
but why wouldn't you?

0:03:47.674,0:03:49.557
Still, no fun to feel like a bully.

0:03:51.513,0:03:55.757
To be clear, this video is not
here to prove anyone wrong.

0:03:55.807,0:04:00.118
The game did not land well
with players, and that's OK.

0:04:00.168,0:04:03.934
I have no right to tell
players how they are

0:04:03.984,0:04:06.913
supposed to have fun playing board games.

0:04:11.717,0:04:13.871
The board is a hexagon of 91 cells.

0:04:13.921,0:04:17.389
Each player has a set
of pieces made of hexes:

0:04:17.439,0:04:22.467
a domino, two shapes of three,
and four shapes of four.

0:04:22.950,0:04:26.826
The first player, red, also
gets this: a single hex.

0:04:26.876,0:04:29.797
Red places it anywhere to start the game.

0:04:29.847,0:04:34.488
It is such a tiny move that it
does not really count as a turn,

0:04:34.538,0:04:37.810
which is why we say each
player then gets seven turns.

0:04:38.284,0:04:41.712
So red owns 25 cells of
material and blue owns 24.

0:04:41.762,0:04:46.184
Remember that difference;
the players certainly did.

0:04:46.500,0:04:51.370
On your turn you place one of your pieces,
and there are exactly two ways to do it.

0:04:51.850,0:04:54.063
One: start a new snake.

0:04:54.113,0:04:58.580
Then the piece must not touch any
other piece at all, of either colour.

0:04:59.050,0:04:59.561
Two: grow.

0:04:59.611,0:05:03.261
The piece touches at least
one of your own pieces.

0:05:03.311,0:05:05.996
It is allowed to touch
the opponent as well,

0:05:06.046,0:05:08.590
and it may join two of
your snakes together.

0:05:09.067,0:05:12.317
Whatever you do, your cells
must stay snake-shaped:

0:05:12.367,0:05:16.184
no cell may have more than
two of your own neighbours,

0:05:16.234,0:05:20.427
and three of your cells may
never all touch each other.

0:05:20.477,0:05:23.225
No branches, no blobs, no sharp turns.

0:05:23.275,0:05:27.677
Every group of your cells
is a path, or a closed ring.

0:05:28.150,0:05:30.727
If you cannot place anything, you pass.

0:05:30.777,0:05:33.751
When neither player can
move, the game ends.

0:05:33.801,0:05:38.441
You measure your longest snake in
cells, and the longer snake wins.

0:05:38.491,0:05:40.020
Equal lengths are a tie.

0:05:40.484,0:05:41.964
That's the whole rulebook.

0:05:44.400,0:05:48.596
The rules are short, but they
hide a surprising amount of play.

0:05:48.646,0:05:53.560
Three ideas carry most of it, and all
three show up in the game we just watched.

0:05:56.233,0:05:58.627
The most direct weapon is a wall.

0:05:58.677,0:06:01.189
Red has just started a second snake.

0:06:01.239,0:06:04.670
Blue answers with a U-shaped piece
right against one of its ends,

0:06:04.720,0:06:09.583
and that end is dead: there is
nowhere left for it to grow.

0:06:10.040,0:06:13.465
Starting a new snake
looks like a wasted move,

0:06:13.515,0:06:17.120
because it adds nothing
to your longest snake.

0:06:17.170,0:06:19.558
But it can be an investment.

0:06:19.608,0:06:23.494
Red's second snake started
far away from its first,

0:06:23.544,0:06:27.222
and on move nine a single
two-piece joins the two:

0:06:27.272,0:06:29.900
seven cells become fourteen in one move.

0:06:30.367,0:06:32.469
Walls also work the other way round.

0:06:33.049,0:06:37.003
The cells right next to the middle
of a snake are lost for its owner,

0:06:37.053,0:06:40.676
since a piece there would give
a body cell a third neighbour,

0:06:40.726,0:06:46.550
but for the opponent they are perfectly
good: reserved, to be used whenever needed.

0:06:46.600,0:06:50.123
In this game, two cells beside
red's snake, up in the corner,

0:06:50.173,0:06:55.187
belong to blue from now on, and blue's
very last piece goes straight into them.

0:06:55.237,0:07:00.112
As the board fills, these dead cells spread
and split the free space into pockets.

0:07:00.162,0:07:03.265
Late in the game the question
is not how many pieces you have,

0:07:03.315,0:07:06.097
but how many cells you can still reach.

0:07:12.133,0:07:15.688
Throughout the years, I focused
on other projects at CGE:

0:07:15.738,0:07:18.628
joining the Arnak team
for all the expansions,

0:07:18.678,0:07:21.419
working on SETI and its
expansion, and more.

0:07:21.469,0:07:25.063
But this game stayed in the
back of my mind all those years.

0:07:25.783,0:07:29.107
Snekagon is just seven turns
on a fairly small board.

0:07:29.157,0:07:32.394
It looks like a game that
can be solved easily.

0:07:32.444,0:07:35.941
I gave it a few shots, and always failed.

0:07:36.475,0:07:40.992
I tried some basic minimax search
algorithms that I had a general idea about,

0:07:41.042,0:07:44.677
but that was just not
enough to tackle this,

0:07:44.727,0:07:49.670
and long discussions with strong players
of abstract games did not crack it either.

0:07:49.720,0:07:54.583
At that point, I knew this would
be quite a problem to solve.

0:07:55.666,0:07:59.282
Meanwhile, the advent of large
language models was unfolding.

0:07:59.817,0:08:04.445
Of course I asked them to solve
the game, and none of them could.

0:08:04.495,0:08:08.036
Then Opus 5.5 came out, and I
decided to try one more time.

0:08:08.086,0:08:14.676
Not by asking for the answer, but by
asking it to build the tools to find one.

0:08:16.850,0:08:18.759
The first step was not solving the game.

0:08:18.809,0:08:21.889
It was getting a program
that plays it well.

0:08:21.939,0:08:24.230
In the first session we
had a fast rules engine,

0:08:24.280,0:08:27.540
a web board to play on,
and a first opponent.

0:08:28.550,0:08:31.058
That first opponent used
Monte Carlo tree search.

0:08:31.108,0:08:33.860
It plays thousands of
random games from each

0:08:33.910,0:08:36.462
position and prefers
moves that tend to win.

0:08:36.512,0:08:40.674
It sounds clever, but I
beat it comfortably as blue.

0:08:40.724,0:08:45.164
Random games simply do not understand
walls: out of two thousand legal moves,

0:08:45.214,0:08:48.574
often only a handful really
matter, usually walls,

0:08:48.624,0:08:52.087
and random games cannot tell
them apart from the rest.

0:08:52.137,0:08:55.188
It let itself get boxed
in again and again.

0:08:55.238,0:08:59.580
It turned out that the tree is simply
too wide for Monte Carlo to be effective.

0:09:00.667,0:09:04.390
The second engine looked ahead
properly, with alpha-beta search,

0:09:04.684,0:09:07.548
but judged positions
with a hand-made rule,

0:09:07.598,0:09:11.747
and it did even worse: it lost
to the random games, 2 to 10.

0:09:13.033,0:09:17.342
So instead of guessing what
matters, we let the game tell us.

0:09:17.392,0:09:20.552
The exact solver, which
we will meet in a moment,

0:09:20.602,0:09:23.537
can settle positions late in the game.

0:09:23.587,0:09:28.081
We solved tens of thousands of such
positions and fitted a simple model,

0:09:28.131,0:09:33.566
a logistic regression over 13 features
of the position, to predict who wins.

0:09:33.616,0:09:38.930
The features are simple things: the
length of each player's longest snake,

0:09:38.980,0:09:41.545
how many snake ends can still grow,

0:09:41.595,0:09:47.284
who reaches which empty cells first, or
how many separate snakes each player has.

0:09:47.334,0:09:52.898
With that learned judgement the engine beat
the random-playout engine 16 games to 0,

0:09:52.948,0:09:55.393
and it beats me comfortably every time.

0:09:59.217,0:10:00.973
Playing well is one thing.

0:10:01.023,0:10:03.175
Solving means something much stronger:

0:10:03.474,0:10:07.057
proving the result of perfect
play, with no guessing anywhere.

0:10:08.134,0:10:09.272
How big is the tree?

0:10:09.322,0:10:11.871
First, the cheap trick.

0:10:11.921,0:10:16.111
The board looks the same after
six rotations and a mirror,

0:10:16.161,0:10:19.219
so up to twelve positions are really one.

0:10:19.269,0:10:23.622
That leaves red just 12 truly
different cells for the 1-hex,

0:10:23.672,0:10:28.894
and after the centre opening, blue has 172
truly different replies instead of 2,132.

0:10:28.944,0:10:32.478
But symmetry only helps at the very start.

0:10:32.528,0:10:38.807
A few moves in, positions are almost never
symmetric, and the numbers keep coming:

0:10:38.857,0:10:43.606
around 1,600 replies for red,
around 900 for blue after that.

0:10:43.656,0:10:47.224
They shrink as the board
fills, but they multiply.

0:10:47.274,0:10:51.017
Even counting rotated and
mirrored games as one,

0:10:51.067,0:10:53.738
sampling random games suggests something

0:10:53.788,0:10:56.286
like 10^29 different
ways a game can unfold.

0:10:56.336,0:10:59.514
You have no chance to look at them all.

0:11:00.584,0:11:04.158
So the solver has to be clever
about which parts it never looks at.

0:11:04.537,0:11:06.694
Four tricks do almost all of the work.

0:11:07.167,0:11:07.759
One, speed.

0:11:07.809,0:11:12.151
The whole board fits into a single
128-bit number, one bit per cell.

0:11:12.201,0:11:16.935
Every position the solver meets
becomes a pair of such numbers,

0:11:16.985,0:11:19.350
one for red and one for blue.

0:11:19.400,0:11:23.731
Finding all the neighbours of a
snake is a handful of bit shifts,

0:11:23.781,0:11:28.167
and the legality of a piece in every
possible spot is checked at once.

0:11:29.200,0:11:29.826
Two, memory.

0:11:29.876,0:11:33.334
Different move orders often
reach the same position,

0:11:33.384,0:11:35.610
so every result is stored and reused.

0:11:36.684,0:11:37.372
Three, bounds.

0:11:37.422,0:11:39.597
This one is specific to Snekagon.

0:11:39.647,0:11:44.462
A snake never gets shorter, so its
current length is a sure lower bound.

0:11:44.512,0:11:47.152
And we can compute a sure upper bound:

0:11:47.202,0:11:52.845
the cells a player can still reach,
filled with the pieces they still have.

0:11:52.895,0:11:56.220
If blue is already guaranteed
more than red could ever get,

0:11:56.270,0:11:59.374
the game is over, even
with pieces left to play.

0:12:00.434,0:12:01.019
Four, order.

0:12:01.069,0:12:04.429
The solver only needs one
winning move to prove a win,

0:12:04.479,0:12:07.090
so it tries the most
promising moves first,

0:12:07.140,0:12:10.374
ranked by the same learned
judgement that powers the engine.

0:12:11.434,0:12:15.999
With all of that, the first real
test: a position after four moves.

0:12:16.049,0:12:19.227
The solver checked every one
of red's 956 distinct moves,

0:12:19.277,0:12:24.443
11 billion positions in total, and
proved that red loses after each of them.

0:12:24.493,0:12:28.148
That took about ten hours
of single-core computing.

0:12:28.198,0:12:32.114
For one position, four moves
into a fifteen-move game.

0:12:34.713,0:12:38.546
Solving the whole game
at once was out of reach,

0:12:38.596,0:12:40.919
so we picked one line
and tried to prove it.

0:12:40.969,0:12:43.717
Red opens in the centre.

0:12:43.767,0:12:47.787
The engine's favourite answer for
blue is this four-piece at the top,

0:12:47.837,0:12:52.776
which it rates at about 65 % for blue;
the next best reply sits around 50 %.

0:12:52.826,0:12:56.138
This was our first
glimpse of a real result,

0:12:56.188,0:12:59.202
and it goes straight
against what players think:

0:12:59.252,0:13:05.214
they say the first player wins, but it
may very well be the other way around.

0:13:05.264,0:13:08.053
It seemed that blue
had the better chances,

0:13:08.103,0:13:12.093
so we tried to actually prove that
blue always wins with perfect play.

0:13:13.116,0:13:14.733
What does that take?

0:13:14.783,0:13:17.143
Red has 1,589 genuinely different replies.

0:13:17.193,0:13:20.910
For every single one of them
we need one blue answer,

0:13:20.960,0:13:24.066
and then a proof that
every red follow-up loses.

0:13:24.116,0:13:27.931
Each of those follow-ups is
a position after five moves,

0:13:27.981,0:13:33.346
a job one processor core can finish
in a few minutes, or in a few hours.

0:13:34.416,0:13:37.656
So I put my home laptop on
it, working day and night.

0:13:39.016,0:13:40.382
And it worked.

0:13:40.432,0:13:42.931
Red reply after red reply fell,

0:13:42.981,0:13:47.888
almost always to the very first
blue answer the engine suggested.

0:13:47.938,0:13:50.655
Sixteen replies proven in a week,

0:13:50.705,0:13:56.713
each costing between 13 and 241 hours
of computing, about 60 on average.

0:13:56.763,0:14:00.888
That sounds like progress,
until you do the division:

0:14:00.938,0:14:03.269
sixteen out of 1,589 is one percent.

0:14:03.319,0:14:09.796
At that pace, this one opening line would
keep my laptop busy for a year or two.

0:14:12.050,0:14:15.010
The toughest of the sixteen
took 241 hours on its own.

0:14:15.060,0:14:18.361
Red answers with a small
bent three-piece that

0:14:18.411,0:14:20.933
runs from the centre
straight at blue's snake,

0:14:20.983,0:14:23.402
and blue answers in the top corner.

0:14:23.452,0:14:28.543
Red grows its snake, and from there
both sides play for every cell.

0:14:28.593,0:14:32.382
Each of them builds a second snake.

0:14:32.432,0:14:36.441
Then blue plays a straight
four right down the middle,

0:14:36.491,0:14:41.566
between red's two snakes, and it
joins blue's own two snakes into one.

0:14:41.616,0:14:43.010
Blue wins, 21 to 15.

0:14:47.500,0:14:50.261
It started to look like
blue actually wins.

0:14:50.311,0:14:51.949
But then we hit this red reply.

0:14:51.999,0:14:55.294
Red plays a small bent
three-piece that runs its

0:14:55.344,0:14:58.949
snake from the centre
straight towards blue's snake.

0:14:58.999,0:15:01.639
The solver tried blue's
40 most promising answers.

0:15:01.689,0:15:02.920
All 40 failed.

0:15:02.970,0:15:05.968
Not by guesswork: for each one,

0:15:06.018,0:15:10.010
the solver found a red reply and
proved that blue cannot win after it.

0:15:10.483,0:15:13.983
In 31 of the 40 cases,
blue actually loses.

0:15:16.683,0:15:20.063
In the other 9, red's
reply holds blue to a draw.

0:15:23.283,0:15:28.023
That one red reply alone
ate 282 hours of computing.

0:15:29.650,0:15:35.266
Let's look at the very first answer blue
tried, because it is the most natural one.

0:15:35.316,0:15:37.605
Blue wraps a U around red's starting hex.

0:15:37.655,0:15:40.680
The centre end of red's snake is dead.

0:15:41.150,0:15:43.426
Red answers by growing from the other end,

0:15:43.476,0:15:47.132
and the solver proves that this
position is exactly a draw.

0:15:47.182,0:15:51.850
With perfect play from here,
neither side can force a win.

0:15:52.317,0:15:54.071
Red's answer is quietly brutal.

0:15:54.121,0:15:57.185
Red now has these spaces reserved.

0:15:57.235,0:16:00.684
Red has a free road into
the bottom of the board.

0:16:00.734,0:16:03.667
The natural answer is to wall it off,

0:16:03.717,0:16:09.315
right under red's two cells, and
the engine rates that highly too.

0:16:09.365,0:16:13.927
But red simply turns the other way
and flows around the wall to the left,

0:16:13.977,0:16:15.977
and blue loses, 15 to 16.

0:16:16.400,0:16:19.094
So here is a challenge for you.

0:16:19.144,0:16:23.280
Pause the video right here, and try to
find a move for blue that does not lose,

0:16:23.330,0:16:25.759
or at least one that feels strong.

0:16:26.176,0:16:27.176
Ready?

0:16:28.273,0:16:29.273
Three.

0:16:29.640,0:16:30.640
Two.

0:16:30.933,0:16:31.933
One.

0:16:32.233,0:16:33.844
Here it is.

0:16:33.894,0:16:35.426
Did you get it?

0:16:35.476,0:16:37.478
If not, don't feel bad.

0:16:37.528,0:16:40.631
The engine, which beats
most humans comfortably,

0:16:40.681,0:16:45.123
ranked this move only 76th, and
all 75 moves it liked better lose.

0:16:46.050,0:16:47.595
And here is why the 76th works.

0:16:47.645,0:16:52.811
Blue starts its second snake not in the
open space at the bottom, but on the left,

0:16:52.861,0:16:55.413
close enough to the top
end of its first snake

0:16:55.463,0:16:58.636
that a single three-piece can join them.

0:16:58.686,0:17:02.795
Red, though, still threatens to
grow into the bottom of the board.

0:17:02.845,0:17:07.233
Red does flow into its two cells and
cuts one end of the new snake off.

0:17:07.283,0:17:13.290
Blue's next piece, an L at the bottom,
looks almost random, but it is not.

0:17:13.340,0:17:16.481
Of blue's 159 possible moves
here, only 21 keep the draw,

0:17:16.531,0:17:20.070
and every single one of them goes
into the bottom of the board:

0:17:20.120,0:17:23.182
blue has to claim that
space before red does.

0:17:23.232,0:17:28.720
Then blue's three-piece joins the two
snakes: eight cells become fifteen.

0:17:29.833,0:17:33.513
From there, the game ends in a
draw, seventeen to seventeen.

0:17:34.600,0:17:38.093
Think about what that
means for a human player.

0:17:38.143,0:17:41.533
The move that saves the game is one
that a strong engine ranks 76th.

0:17:41.583,0:17:45.125
A person sitting at the table
would almost never find it,

0:17:45.175,0:17:47.670
and would never know it was there.

0:17:50.283,0:17:51.791
So, why is it so hard to prove?

0:17:51.841,0:17:57.303
A proof of a win has a lopsided shape,
and that shape is the whole problem.

0:17:58.240,0:18:02.537
To prove that blue wins, blue needs
just one good move at each of her turns.

0:18:02.587,0:18:06.201
But she has to answer every
single move red could make.

0:18:06.251,0:18:09.911
One branch, then all
branches, then one, then all.

0:18:09.961,0:18:13.661
The "one" levels are
cheap if you guess right.

0:18:13.711,0:18:15.430
The "all" levels never are.

0:18:16.483,0:18:19.666
And guessing right is
exactly what failed us.

0:18:19.716,0:18:23.433
Under that stubborn red reply,
blue has 1,032 different moves.

0:18:23.483,0:18:28.940
To prove that blue cannot win there,
we have to refute every one of them.

0:18:28.990,0:18:31.413
The 40 most promising
are done; 992 are left.

0:18:31.463,0:18:37.193
Finishing them all would take my laptop
anywhere from a few days to a few weeks.

0:18:38.066,0:18:39.396
Scale that up.

0:18:39.446,0:18:42.025
If the centre opening is a blue win,

0:18:42.075,0:18:47.341
proving it at the pace we measured is
a job of a year or two on my laptop.

0:18:47.391,0:18:50.741
Proving the result of the
whole game means doing that for

0:18:50.791,0:18:55.725
every one of red's 12 different
first moves: a decade or more.

0:18:55.775,0:18:59.892
And if the true answer is
a draw, it is worse still:

0:18:59.942,0:19:02.715
a draw has to be proven from both sides,

0:19:02.765,0:19:05.996
so the "all moves" levels
appear for both players.

0:19:07.050,0:19:11.852
The deepest reason why this game is so
hard to solve is the one we saw at move 76.

0:19:11.902,0:19:16.492
The solver is fast because good
guesses let it skip almost everything.

0:19:16.542,0:19:20.383
In this line, the decisive move
was one nobody would guess,

0:19:20.433,0:19:24.485
and nothing tells us it is the only
place where the game hides one.

0:19:24.535,0:19:29.528
And that is exactly why we need to
prove every single one of red's replies:

0:19:29.578,0:19:34.840
we never know where a move that is
rated weak is actually the strongest.

0:19:41.047,0:19:44.120
So why did players feel that
the first player cannot lose?

0:19:44.170,0:19:47.477
I think the answer is
sitting right on the table.

0:19:47.527,0:19:52.037
The starting player takes the
center, and has one more piece.

0:19:52.150,0:19:55.798
If both players simply build their
own snakes as long as they can,

0:19:55.848,0:19:57.904
red wins by exactly one cell.

0:19:57.954,0:20:04.705
That is the game most people play at first,
and in that game the feeling is correct.

0:20:04.755,0:20:09.790
Red also moves first and last, so it
always seems to have the last word.

0:20:10.034,0:20:14.207
What the numbers show is a different
game, one where blue blocks.

0:20:14.257,0:20:16.555
In the opening line we studied,

0:20:16.605,0:20:20.394
blue wins by force against 16 of
the 17 red replies we finished,

0:20:20.444,0:20:23.962
usually with the very first
move the engine suggests.

0:20:24.012,0:20:29.238
Against the 17th it is the other way
round: blue is the one clinging on,

0:20:29.288,0:20:32.974
and in the line we followed only
a move ranked 76th saved her.

0:20:34.034,0:20:35.282
So were the players wrong?

0:20:35.332,0:20:37.369
Not really.

0:20:37.419,0:20:39.273
A game is not its game tree.

0:20:39.323,0:20:42.013
A game is what people
experience at the table,

0:20:42.063,0:20:45.541
and if a game feels decided,
it is decided for them.

0:20:45.591,0:20:50.388
Theoretical balance means nothing when
the balancing moves are invisible.

0:20:50.438,0:20:52.514
Let me say that again.

0:20:52.564,0:20:56.694
Theoretical balance means nothing when
the balancing moves are invisible.

0:21:00.317,0:21:05.485
And just because a game is perfectly
balanced, that does not guarantee it is fun.

0:21:05.535,0:21:08.705
What makes a game fun is a
whole other big open question,

0:21:08.925,0:21:12.397
one that game designers have been
trying to answer for decades.

0:21:14.134,0:21:17.414
And that leaves me with a question
I find genuinely interesting.

0:21:17.960,0:21:21.263
Is a game where every result hangs
on a single hidden cell a deep game,

0:21:21.313,0:21:22.728
or a frustrating one?

0:21:22.778,0:21:27.655
Perhaps it is both, depending on
who is sitting across from you.

0:21:27.705,0:21:29.983
It almost feels like the
game of Go in some ways:

0:21:30.033,0:21:34.087
you know there is an astronomical
number of possible games,

0:21:34.137,0:21:36.393
and it could hypothetically be calculated.

0:21:36.799,0:21:39.148
But in the end, you
must read your opponent,

0:21:39.295,0:21:42.014
trust your intuition,
and feel the patterns.

0:21:45.417,0:21:46.772
So where does this leave us?

0:21:46.822,0:21:48.977
Three things I am now sure of.

0:21:49.450,0:21:52.502
For human players, the
game is extremely tight.

0:21:52.552,0:21:54.668
Results swing on a single cell,

0:21:54.718,0:21:58.620
and the saving move can be one that
even a strong engine ranks 76th.

0:21:58.900,0:22:00.996
An exact proof is extremely expensive.

0:22:01.046,0:22:05.649
Hundreds of hours of computing
went into a single opening line,

0:22:05.699,0:22:07.540
and that line is still open.

0:22:07.950,0:22:11.125
It is not at all clear how the
game ends with perfect play.

0:22:11.175,0:22:16.410
Blue looked like the favourite,
until one red reply refused to fall.

0:22:17.667,0:22:18.713
Seven turns.

0:22:18.763,0:22:20.124
Ninety-one cells.

0:22:20.174,0:22:22.907
And still, nobody knows who wins.

0:22:26.017,0:22:30.105
If you want to play against the engine
yourself, right in your browser,

0:22:30.155,0:22:33.859
or analyse any position, click
the link in the description.

0:22:33.909,0:22:36.067
To buy the game, visit Maty Moves.

0:22:36.117,0:22:38.332
And maybe one of you will
find the next move 76.

0:22:38.382,0:22:40.557
Thanks for watching.

